From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 01:42:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nak: schedule for memory-level parallelism (NAK_SCHED_MLP)

NAK's prepass scheduler puts a load about ten instructions above its
use, even when the load does not depend on the computation in between.
With few warps, as in a dispatch of one to five workgroups, nothing then
hides the latency. A warp waits for its independent loads one at a time,
and after a barrier's L1 invalidate every one of them is an L2 round
trip. In a shader with an integer chain and 27 independent loads,
NVIDIA's code issues 25 of the loads within its first ~170 instructions
(35 GPRs). NAK's code issues each load right before its use, all into one
register on one scoreboard (24 GPRs), and a 5-workgroup dispatch of it
costs 6.5 us after a barrier against NVIDIA's 2.4 us (RTX 5090).

Causes, and what NAK_SCHED_MLP changes:

- The prepass's list scheduler records when an instruction becomes ready
  (instr_info), but enters it in the future-ready set at the graph's
  static ready cycle, which is 0 in the prepass. So no edge latency ever
  delays anything, and a load becomes ready as soon as its use is placed.
  With the knob, the future-ready set takes the recorded cycle.
- estimate_variable_latency() models a global, local, texture or surface
  access as 32 cycles, an L1 hit. Levels 1-3 model it as 320/240 (1) or
  640/480 (2, 3) cycles (memory / texture and surface).
- Level 3 also hoists: while registers are below the scheduling threshold,
  the prepass (and the post-RA scheduler) prefer every other instruction,
  ready now or soon, over a memory access. Both walk backwards, so the
  accesses rise as far as their operands allow.
- Read-only accesses (constant-order loads, texture reads) are not ordered
  against the shader's other memory operations in the prepass graph.
  Barriers still order them.
- When all six scoreboards are busy, a variable-latency instruction shares
  one (as NAK_BAR_SHARE) instead of waiting for the oldest.
- Occupancy: the occupancy target (NAK_OCC_TARGET) is chosen on the plain
  schedule, and the shader is then scheduled again for that target with
  loads in flight. A unit whose MLP schedule does not fit the register
  target falls back to the plain schedule for the same target, so the
  knob does not cross an occupancy cliff.

The knob (0-3, default 0) is part of nak_knobs_key() (bits 18-19) and so
of NVK's shader cache key.

Offline (drm-shim, GB202), the test shader at level 3 has 28 of 28 loads
in flight at 40 GPRs and 48 warps, against 1 of 28 at level 0. On the
Steel Nomad corpus (115 compute, 136 fragment, 41 vertex shaders),
level 3 against level 0: instructions +0.8% (compute), +0.1% (fragment),
+3% (vertex), from register copies around vector destinations; memory
spills 8 -> 2 (compute) and 14 -> 30 (fragment); average warps per SM
41.5 -> 41.9 (compute) and unchanged elsewhere.
